From 22e47eb5923a2ba7e11d62085a7fca2221b02b18 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivela=CC=88?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 18:34:17 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 14/15] extensions: serve a web accessible resource to a page

A page could not load a resource an extension declared web accessible.
The request never reached the browser as itself: the renderer rewrote
it to chrome-extension://invalid/, which is what the extensions code
does when it decides a page may not ask.

Two copies of the policy that makes that decision. The base
ExtensionsRendererClient owns one and is told which extensions have
loaded, through OnExtensionLoaded, and answers WillSendRequest from it.
QtWebEngine kept a second one of its own, from before the base class
had one, and overrode WillSendRequest to use it. Nothing ever told that
copy about an extension, so its set of ids with web accessible
resources was permanently empty and every request was refused.

The copy is gone and the base class answers. Its WillSendRequest also
takes the upstream URL, which Qt's caller already had and dropped, so a
resource reached through a redirect is now allowed by the extension
that redirected to it.

It stops a whole class of extension working rather than one API. A
content script small enough to declare statically imports the bundle
that does the work; that import is a subresource, so the bundle never
arrived and nothing the extension meant to do in the page happened.
1Password fills this way; Bitwarden declares its scripts statically,
which is why it was unaffected and this went unseen.
